			<div class="content" id="post-content-1419262">
									

<blockquote>
<p>1) I can't see how that would lead to the &quot;edge of the world&quot; sort of thing as you present it. That is, there being some sort of illusion which covers up the edge until you go past it, being unable to return once past the edge, and there being air and sunlight (but nothing solid) present beyond the edge.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My thinking on that point was sort of related to what happens when you clip past the edge of the world in a video game - you might encounter junk data and nonsense geography for awhile, or you might end up in a bottomless void.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>2) Similarly, I can't see how that would lead to copied animal life all becoming nocturnal, aggressive, and impossible to find during the day.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>3) Buildings are either copied perfectly or copied such that have noticeably bizarre design and strange looking furniture. If the same happened when copying plant life the results would either have no mutations or be completely nonviable; likewise, copied computers would either be copied perfectly or be completely non-functional. On the other hand, copied plants having lots of mutations suggests that copying causes lots of little mistakes rather than a few large scale mistakes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think these go hand-in-hand. The fact that there's so little observable life would probably indicate that most of the &quot;copies&quot; that end up there are nonviable. I didn't go into a lot of description of the plant life, but I think it can be assumed that the plants that are there are stunted and twisted. Since they probably didn't &quot;grow&quot; there, it could be assumed that they came into being in a mutated state that wouldn't have been viable if they'd grown from seed. I didn't want to really go into too many specifics except to say that the objects were &quot;wrong&quot;.</p>
<p>I didn't mean to particularly imply that the lifeforms are nocturnal, just that they're rare and hard to find.</p>

